abstract: "Proves the Luner-Grimmer conjecture (arXiv:2410.14811 v1/v2, Conjecture 6.2): if two closed convex sets contain a common ball of radius delta and the start lies within R of its centre, the worst-case distance to the intersection after N cycles of alternating projections is max over 0 <= c <= kappa of c^(2N-1)(sqrt(R^2-delta^2) - delta sqrt((1+c)/(1-c))), kappa = 1 - 2 delta^2/R^2, in every dimension. Extends the exact answer to balls of different radii about a common centre, any number (at least two) of single projections and real Hilbert space, and gives the exact worst residual, which recovers the Zamani-Glineur rate as delta -> 0. Includes the sharp asymptotic and practical cycle counts (93 exact versus 833 classical at R/delta = 10, accuracy 1e-3). The formula and extremal half-planes are Luner and Grimmer's; the contribution is the upper bound and the extensions. Standard-library check suites (88,902 explicit checks). Unrefereed candidate; internal AI-assisted review only. Text, data and figures CC0-1.0; code MIT."
